#VIVA
The "one session" interview cheat sheet

If the interviewer asks "What is Pydantic?", say:

Pydantic is a Python library used for data parsing and runtime validation based on Python type annotations.
In FastAPI, it is commonly used to define request and response schemas. It validates incoming data,
converts compatible values when appropriate, and provides structured validation errors.

If they ask "Why FastAPI uses it?"

It allows us to define the expected API schema as a Python model,
and FastAPI uses that model to validate request bodies and generate API documentation automatically.

"What is BaseModel?"

BaseModel is Pydantic's base class. By inheriting from it, our class gets Pydantic's parsing, validation,
and serialization behavior.

"Pydantic vs type hints?"

Type hints alone generally don't perform runtime validation. 
Pydantic uses type hints as the basis for runtime parsing and validation.

"Pydantic vs dataclass?"

Dataclasses primarily provide a convenient way to create structured Python objects,
whereas Pydantic additionally provides runtime validation, parsing, and serialization, 
which makes it particularly useful at API boundaries.


SQLite can handle surprisingly large databases. 
The limitation is often not the database size, but how many users/processes need to access it simultaneously.
SQLite allows many readers, but writes are serialized—effectively, only one writer can modify the database at a time



